### VietPay — Auth flows (user-service via Traefik)
### VS Code REST Client. Run `login` first; later requests read its response
### inline via {{login.response.body.accessToken}} (no separate @-assignments,
### which would otherwise be slurped into the request body).
### IntelliJ HTTP Client: use {{login.response.body.$.accessToken}} instead.

@baseUrl = http://localhost
@email = dajneem235@yopmail.com
@password = Password123!

### Register a new user -> 201, returns access + refresh tokens
# @name register
POST {{baseUrl}}/auth/register
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "email": "{{email}}",
  "password": "{{password}}",
  "firstName": "Test",
  "lastName": "User"
}

### Login -> 200, returns access + refresh tokens
# @name login
POST {{baseUrl}}/auth/login
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "email": "{{email}}",
  "password": "{{password}}"
}

### Verify (what Traefik ForwardAuth calls) -> 200 + X-User-Id header
GET {{baseUrl}}/auth/verify
Authorization: Bearer {{login.response.body.accessToken}}

### Refresh -> rotate the token pair (old refresh token becomes invalid)
# @name refresh
POST {{baseUrl}}/auth/refresh
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "refreshToken": "{{login.response.body.refreshToken}}"
}

### Logout -> revoke refresh token (DB) + blacklist this access token's jti (Redis)
POST {{baseUrl}}/auth/logout
Authorization: Bearer {{login.response.body.accessToken}}

### After logout, verify must now fail with 401 (jti blacklisted)
GET {{baseUrl}}/auth/verify
Authorization: Bearer {{login.response.body.accessToken}}
